%U A291600 2612299524,1829353999,632270116,3534265881,3534265881,3534265881,3534265881,2878326162,866810261,9561884206,2200341226,484926452
%N A291600 First 10-digit number to appear n times in the decimal expansion of Pi.
%C A291600 This sequence was mentioned in a forum post called "Ten repeating numbers in Pi". It was about finding a 10-digit number that repeated in Pi. The answer was a(2) = A197123(10) = 4392366484.
%C A291600 a(10000) = 1036687475, whose 10000th appearance starts at digit 93872088331591.  There are only 158 distinct values in the first 10000, and 5324965743 is a(n) for 1,679 of the n, including 824 consecutive terms ending at a(6829). - _Jeff Sponaugle_, Oct 19 2026
%H A291600 Jeff Sponaugle, <a href="/A291600/b291600.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A291600 echochamber, <a href="http://echochamber.me/viewtopic.php?t=74216">Ten repeating numbers in Pi</a>, Forums for the webcomic xkcd.com, Sep 2011.
%e A291600 a(2) = 4392366484 because 4392366484 is the first 10-digit number to appear 2 times in the decimal expansion of Pi.
%Y A291600 Cf. A000796, A096567, A197123, A276686, A276992, A276993, A277171, A291599.
